  "live_records": {
    "q-132111": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132111",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14261478/assets/0c93a3a1d720e45d551ba3225b20250d512f219d123d5f9376094daec43c981a.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=-2x,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14261478/assets/ff94fdaad94c49b2cea35ab6ca8a59d7c99bfc58214bc211ed13a792c90a94eb.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 0,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le -2x\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{-2x}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{-2x}f(x,y)\\,dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
