    "q-132113": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132113",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14261478/assets/d594f3356668e3bc605e6655ec92787d9e130be12f3e9e01b9cc482c6897070a.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=x$ and $y={x}^{3},$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14261478/assets/e2d1366028a15314a6ae1041af3a411189553376382ea5477bb16d5d4789bd51.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le x\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{x}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{x}f(x,y)\\,dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
