    "q-80634": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80634",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14261478/assets/a51b5dca6436e6ffd3af0d1cfa801141d97e7660e6218344011a5ac965c722c6.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=2(x-2)^{2}-1$ and $y=2-(x-2)^{2},$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14261478/assets/a15535c1eee33018c0486fd3cf1900fd151a9a23cf374c6a0ccec60ca47c64c3.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,2(x-2)^{2}-1\\le y\\le 2-(x-2)^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{2(x-2)^{2}-1}^{2-(x-2)^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}[{\\int }_{2(x-2)^{2}-1}^{2-(x-2)^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-80634-a",
