      "knowledge_point": "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given",
      "kp_id": "e-7024"
    },
    "q-81332": {
      "dom_id": "step-q81332",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=4,$ and the curve $y={x}^{2}.$ What is ${∬}_{D}(y+x){e}^{x+y}\\,dA$ expressed as a repeated integral?",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14261478/assets/ec35c9763e9af13cd56a8bb8c0466c6cc267d4a4f99fb07d8fdab9f78354139f.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(y+x){e}^{x+y}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}(y+x){e}^{x+y}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-81332-a",
              "type": "math",
              "value": "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{4}^{{x}^{2}}(y+x){e}^{x+y}\\,dy\\,dx",
