    "q-81342": {
      "dom_id": "step-q81342",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=\\frac{3}{2}-x$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2},$ then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14261478/assets/50a6b2d57fe4dbfbef58b49ae51b89f2001035fa896118eec1e3fcabcde626d9.png)\n\nNow, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}-x & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\ 3-2x & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-3 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$-3\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14261478/assets/78e2ec78c9fb00fb56b7f84b2a5dbb96786dd07e3057b894ad2421e89ede29e2.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-3\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{3}{2}-x\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}/2}^{3/2-x}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}/2}^{3/2-x}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-81342-a",
              "type": "math",
